{"name":"Grasshopper Wing","hex":"#87866f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grasshopper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mostly herbivorous insect of the order Orthoptera, noted for its ability to jump long distances and for the habit of some species communicating by stridulation; they are related to but distinct from crickets.","In the strict sense, refers to insects in the suborder Caelifera, particularly those in the family Acrididae."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of a crop, to be ruined or destroyed by grasshoppers.","To move a riverboat over an obstruction, such as a sandbar."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grasshopper"},{"term":"wing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An appendage of an animal's (bird, bat, insect) body that enables it to fly.","A fin at the side of a ray or similar fish."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To injure slightly (as with a gunshot), especially in the wing or arm.","To fly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wing"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grasshopper","blocks":[{"text":"Grasshoppers are a group of insects belonging to the suborder Caelifera. They are amongst what are possibly the most ancient living groups of chewing herbivorous insects, dating back to the early Triassic, around 250 million years ago."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/American_Bird_Grasshopper.jpg/330px-American_Bird_Grasshopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grasshopper","wikidata":"Q18398431"},{"title":"Wing","blocks":[{"text":"A wing is a structure which produces both lift and drag while moving through air. Wings are defined by two shape characteristics, an airfoil section and a planform. Wing efficiency is expressed as lift-to-drag ratio, which compares the benefit of lift with the air resistance of a given wing shape, as it flies. Aerodynamics includes the study of wing performance in air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg/330px-Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing","wikidata":"Q161358"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Fossil_grasshoppers_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC00013.JPG/500px-Fossil_grasshoppers_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC00013.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Fossil_grasshoppers_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC00013.JPG/1280px-Fossil_grasshoppers_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC00013.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Fossil_grasshoppers_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC00013.JPG/500px-Fossil_grasshoppers_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC00013.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fossil grasshoppers at the Royal Ontario Museum","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFossil_grasshoppers_-_Royal_Ontario_Museum_-_DSC00013.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Two_grasshoppers_utah.jpg/500px-Two_grasshoppers_utah.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Two_grasshoppers_utah.jpg/1280px-Two_grasshoppers_utah.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Two_grasshoppers_utah.jpg/500px-Two_grasshoppers_utah.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two differential grasshoppers, with spines along the tibia of the hind legs","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATwo_grasshoppers_utah.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wi